gluten-free diets and products are  everywhere these days as of 2014 an  estimated 3 million people in the US  alone had sworn off gluten so what we  don't know from those numbers is how  many of those people were avoiding  gluten because they heard that the  gluten-free diet is healthy or they  heard that gluten is just bad for  everyone which it's not versus people  who are actually experiencing some kind  of symptom and attributing that to  gluten some people blame gluten for  symptoms like abdominal pain bloating  and fatigue but don't have celiac  disease the autoimmune reaction to this  villainized complex of proteins found in  wheat barley and rye those are the cases  that some doctors and researchers are  really puzzling over ends from trying to  get to the bottom of  [Music]  here's what we know certain researchers  are convinced that some people have an  immune reaction to gluten or to some  other substance in wheat a study  published in 2016  tested blood samples from 80 patients  who had symptoms after eating wheat it  found that these patients had higher  levels of a class of antibodies against  a component of gluten the team also  found elevated levels of two proteins  that indicate an inflammatory response  to bacteria this doesn't necessarily  mean gluten itself was causing illness  but the researchers proposed that some  part of wheat prompts the intestinal  lining to become more permeable then the  wheat components and gut bacteria sneak  past immune cells in the intestinal  tissue make their way to the bloodstream  and liver and cause inflammation but  another camp thinks most patients don't  have an immune reaction to eat instead  they believe that most are really  reacting to an excess of FODMAPs  fermentable all ago die monosaccharides  and polyols so it's this category of  carbohydrates that are not easily  absorbed by the intestines and so they  can ferment in the gut and that can  cause gas and bloating FODMAPs are  widespread in our diets carbohydrates in  wheat called fruit tense could account  for as much as half of a person's fodmap  intake some researchers believe that  many people feel better after  eliminating wheat from their diet not  because of an immune reaction to gluten  or other wee components but because  they're reducing their FODMAPs  proponents of a low fodmap diet say it's  less restrictive than going totally  gluten-free because people can  reintroduce FODMAPs to find a threshold  that keeps their symptoms in check so  this group based at the University of  Oslo in Norway designed the study to  sort of try to tease out the effects of  FODMAPs  versus gluten and what they did was they  recruited people who were already on  free diet and they had them try one of  three different indistinguishable snack  bars that had either pure gluten or a  fodmap called fruit ten or neither a  placebo bar and they would eat one of  the bars for a week report their  abdominal symptoms and then switch until  they had tried all three of the bars  without knowing which was which in the  end FODMAPs caused the worst symptoms by  a small margin and also more than a  third of the people in the study  reported their worst symptoms after the  placebo bar it was a mess so the  researchers concluded that in most cases  FODMAPs were the culprit sort of  generally in the population people were  reacting to FODMAPs not something  specifically in wheat but there's been  skepticism about that study people who  say that well you didn't really recruit  the people who are likely to have an  immune reaction to wheat and not to  fodmap so the case is not closed rather  than one single cause or culprit the  illness is attributed to gluten may  actually be due to multiple nuanced  conditions so how will this affect our  choices at the grocery store and at the  dinner table well don't do anything to  dressed yet experts say if you suspect  you're reacting to wheat see a doctor  and rule out celiac disease before going  gluten-free a dietitian can also help  manage symptoms without cutting out so  many foods that you're missing out on  important nutrients because there aren't  a lot of good answers about the possible  causes of these conditions a lot of  people when they go to the doctor or to  a dietician are still being encouraged  to just experiment try cutting out  gluten and see if it works for them so  that's sort of where we are right now I  think a lot of people are just sort of  figuring out what makes them feel best  [Music]
 

